## Phase 3 — Detection & classification

| Type | What you see | How to confirm |
|---|---|---|
| In-band | Fetched content returned in response | Point at your server (`python3 -m http.server 8080` on a VPS); look for your content in the reply |
| Blind | Nothing returned, request still happens | Interactsh / Burp Collaborator / webhook.site / RequestRepo callback |
| Semi-blind | No body, but timing / status / error differences | Compare open port, closed port, non-routable IP |

```bash
curl -s -i "https://target.com/api/preview?url=http://YOUR_ID.oast.fun/test"
```

Check before getting excited:
- **Is the callback from the target?** Compare callback source IP to your own. Your browser, a public link-preview bot, or a scanner may be the caller.
- **DNS-only vs HTTP.** DNS lookup without an HTTP request is usually Informative. Try ports 8080/8443/8000 or other schemes to see if TCP egress is firewalled.
- **Semi-blind needs consistency.** Repeat each probe several times; one slow response is noise.

Semi-blind probes:
| Test | Observe |
|---|---|
| `http://127.0.0.1:22` | Fast response / SSH banner in error |
| `http://127.0.0.1:65535` | Connection refused / different timing |
| `http://10.0.0.1` | Timeout / longer response |
| `:80` vs `:443` | Size, status, error differences |

After confirming, map reachability **gently**: a handful of common ports on `127.0.0.1` (22, 80, 443, 3306, 5432, 6379, 8080, 8443, 9200, 11211, 27017) and a few gateways (`10.0.0.1`, `172.16.0.1`, `192.168.1.1`). Record status, length and timing per probe. Don't sweep whole subnets unless the program allows it. SSRFmap (github.com/swisskyrepo/SSRFmap) can help with detection.

